    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d538">

                <pb facs="0758" n="744" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg744"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">123 Hurley, Patrick J.: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Appointed Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Hurley</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-12-20T20:00:00-04:00">December 20,
                            1944—8 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1681. I appreciate your reasons for postponing your trip to Washington<anchor
                        n="6" xml:id="d538-f1"/> and, while I regret the delay in receiving a
                    first-hand report from you, I concur in your decision. In the meantime I should
                    appreciate very much receiving by telegraph a strictly confidential summary of
                    the reports on the situation which you had planned to make in person.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stettinius</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d538-f1" n="6" xml:id="d538-f1-content">
                <p>See telegram No. 2031, December 17, 9 a.m., from the Appointed Ambassador in
                    China, <ref target="#pg210">p. 210</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
